    <article class="article-content" style="margin-top: 36px;">

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <h2>What is Unix time?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Unix time</strong> (also frequently referred to as <strong>epoch time</strong>, <strong>POSIX time</strong>, or a <strong>Unix timestamp</strong>) is a universal system for describing points in time. It measures the total number of seconds that have elapsed since the reference instant of <strong>00:00:00 UTC on Thursday, 1 January 1970</strong>. This fixed historical starting point is universally known as the <em>Unix epoch</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Key characteristics of the Unix epoch standard include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Continuous Integer Counting:</strong> In the standard Unix convention, every day is treated as containing exactly 86,400 seconds. Leap seconds are not represented as separate seconds; instead, the timestamp repeats or slews the final second of the day.</li>
        <li><strong>Seconds vs. Milliseconds:</strong> Traditional operating systems, databases, and APIs (such as Linux, POSIX C libraries, and Python's <code>time.time()</code>) represent timestamps in <strong>seconds</strong> (typically 10 digits for modern dates). However, modern web environments—most notably JavaScript's <code>Date.now()</code> and MongoDB ObjectIDs—store timestamps in <strong>milliseconds</strong> (typically 13 digits for modern dates).</li>
        <li><strong>Negative Timestamps:</strong> Moments occurring prior to 1 January 1970 are represented by negative integer values. For example, <code>-86400</code> corresponds to 31 December 1969 at 00:00:00 UTC.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because different APIs and programming languages alternate between seconds and milliseconds, our converter provides a dedicated unit toggle so you never accidentally misinterpret a millisecond timestamp as seconds or vice versa.
      </p>

      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <h2>Epoch time, UTC, and local time</h2>
      <p>
        A fundamental advantage of Unix time is that a single numeric value represents <strong>one identical, simultaneous instant everywhere in the universe</strong>. Whether an event occurs in London, Tokyo, or New York, the Unix timestamp recorded by synchronized systems is exactly the same.
      </p>
      <p>
        What changes across regions is purely the local calendar date and wall-clock representation:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>UTC (Coordinated Universal Time):</strong> Serves as the global civil time standard without daylight saving adjustments. For example, timestamp <code>1767225600</code> corresponds to <code>1 January 2026, 00:00:00 UTC</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Visitor's Local Time:</strong> Your browser automatically maps the UTC instant to your physical device's configured time zone, displaying your exact local date, hour, and regional offset.</li>
        <li><strong>Eastern Time (America/New_York):</strong> New York and the US East Coast oscillate seasonally between Eastern Standard Time (<strong>EST</strong>, UTC−5) in the winter and Eastern Daylight Time (<strong>EDT</strong>, UTC−4) in the summer. For timestamp <code>1767225600</code>, Eastern Time is <code>31 December 2025, 19:00:00 EST</code>—falling on the preceding calendar day! For timestamp <code>1792918800</code>, Eastern Time evaluates to <code>25 October 2026, 05:00:00 EDT</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Our converter dynamically calculates the correct historical or future abbreviation (EST vs. EDT) for the chosen moment using official IANA timezone rules. If you need to compare this instant across multiple global offices, you can explore our <a href="/compare-time-zones/">Compare Time Zones</a> tool or check current offsets with our <a href="/">GMT to EST Converter</a>. For calculating spans between timestamps, try our <a href="/time-duration-calculator/">Time Duration Calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <!-- Educational Timeline Diagram -->
      <figure style="margin: 32px 0 36px; text-align: center;">
        <img
          src="/assets/epoch-time-explained.webp"
          alt="Timeline from 1 January 1970 to a modern epoch timestamp"
          width="800"
