        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <h2>What about GPS timestamps?</h2>
      <p>
        A frequent point of confusion in aerospace, marine navigation, and IoT telematics is whether a GPS timestamp is interchangeable with a Unix epoch timestamp.
      </p>
      <p>
        The answer is that <strong>GPS time does not handle leap seconds in the same way as UTC</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Different Epoch Origin:</strong> GPS time began at <code>00:00:00 UTC on Sunday, 6 January 1980</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>No Leap Second Adjustments:</strong> While UTC introduces leap seconds periodically to stay aligned with the Earth's slowing rotation, the atomic clock constellation running the Global Positioning System (GPS) never inserts leap seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Cumulative Drift:</strong> Because UTC has accumulated multiple leap seconds since 1980 while GPS time has not, raw GPS time is ahead of UTC by an exact integer number of leap seconds (and differs by a fixed offset from the 1970 Unix epoch).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Therefore, you should always check whether your hardware receiver, telemetry payload, or database provides raw GPS time or if its firmware has already normalized the data into standard UTC or Unix epoch seconds. This converter accepts standard Unix epoch timestamps in seconds or milliseconds.
      </p>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
